 "authorLine": "Agent run r5, 2026-10-25",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: once a government had taken power by force, the Department's working test was control of the country, the people's acceptance and the ability to meet international obligations; it departed from that test on purpose in Wilson's requirement of a constitutional election (1913 to 1920), in the Central American treaty rule it followed until 1936, and in wartime consultation over Argentina and Bolivia (1943 to 1944). What it asked was usually assurances on obligations, sometimes an election, a property or financial guarantee, civil liberties or proof of loyalty to the Allied cause; from 1948 recognition was separated from approval. Where FRUS points for the rest: the printed papers came almost entirely from the State Department's central decimal file (Record Group 59), whose National Archives identifiers are not in the bundled data; the one lot cited that resolves, the Policy Planning Staff's Lot 64 D 563 (Record Group 59), is divided among twelve series dated 1947 to 1962, all unrestricted where checked; the editors' footnotes cite unprinted central-file numbers in only about one document in thirty. What this request cannot get from FRUS: the Department's unprinted deliberations, the other governments' own reasoning, the terms on which relations with Peru's junta resumed in 1962, and anything before 1877 or after 1979, which I did not search.",
